Task 2: Creating Linux Machine Credentials
Subtask 2.1: Generate SSH Key Pair
First, let's create an SSH key pair for secure authentication:

Open a terminal on your control machine
Generate a new SSH key pair:
# Generate SSH key pair
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -C "ansible-automation" -f ~/.ssh/ansible_key

# Set appropriate permissions
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/ansible_key
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub
Display the public key content:
cat ~/.ssh/ansible_key.pub
Copy the public key output for later use
Subtask 2.2: Configure Target Linux Machine
Access your target Linux machine
Add the public key to the authorized_keys file:
# Create .ssh directory if it doesn't exist
mkdir -p ~/.ssh
chmod 700 ~/.ssh

# Add the public key to authorized_keys
echo "your-public-key-content-here" >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Ensure SSH service is running:
# Check SSH service status
sudo systemctl status sshd

# Start SSH service if not running
sudo systemctl start sshd
sudo systemctl enable sshd
Subtask 2.3: Create Linux Credential in Controller
In the Automation Controller web interface, navigate to Resources → Credentials
Click the Add button
Fill in the credential details:
Basic Information:

Name: Linux-SSH-Credential
Description: SSH key-based credential for Linux machines
Organization: Select your organization
Credential Type: Machine
Type Details:

Username: ansible (or your target machine username)
SSH Private Key: Paste the content of your private key
# To get private key content
cat ~/.ssh/ansible_key
Privilege Escalation Method: sudo
Privilege Escalation Username: root
Click Save
Subtask 2.4: Test Linux Credential
Create a simple test playbook to verify the credential:

Navigate to Resources → Templates
Click Add → Add job template
Configure the template:
Template Configuration:

Name: Test-Linux-Credential
Job Type: Run
Inventory: Select your Linux inventory
Project: Create or select a project
Playbook: test-linux-connection.yml
Credentials: Select Linux-SSH-Credential
Create the test playbook content:
---
- name: Test Linux Machine Credential
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  tasks:
    - name: Check system information
      debug:
        msg: "Connected to {{ ansible_hostname }} running {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}"
    
    - name: Test file creation with sudo
      become: yes
      file:
        path: /tmp/ansible_test_file
        state: touch
        mode: '0644'
      register: file_result
    
    - name: Display file creation result
      debug:
        msg: "File creation successful: {{ file_result.changed }}"
    
    - name: Clean up test file
      become: yes
      file:
        path: /tmp/ansible_test_file
        state: absent
Save and launch the job template
Task 3: Creating Windows Machine Credentials
Subtask 3.1: Configure Windows Target Machine
Access your Windows target machine
Enable WinRM service:
# Run as Administrator
# Enable WinRM
winrm quickconfig -q

# Configure WinRM for Ansible
winrm set winrm/config/service/auth '@{Basic="true"}'
winrm set winrm/config/service '@{AllowUnencrypted="true"}'
winrm set winrm/config/winrs '@{MaxMemoryPerShellMB="1024"}'

# Create firewall rule
netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="WinRM-HTTP" dir=in localport=5985 protocol=TCP action=allow
Create a local user for Ansible (if needed):
# Create new user
net user ansible P@ssw0rd123! /add

# Add to administrators group
net localgroup administrators ansible /add
Subtask 3.2: Install Windows Collections
On your Automation Controller, ensure Windows collections are installed:

# Install Windows collection
